<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Extra Info.plist keys, merged into the one the Tauri CLI generates
  (https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/macos-application-bundle/). Keep this file to
  keys Tauri does not already emit — overwriting its values, the version above
  all, causes exactly the conflicts that document warns about.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription is the whole reason this file exists. Lilypad's
  first and best path is the LAN one, and on macOS 15 that path is gated behind
  Local Network privacy. Apple's TN3179 lists the operations that need it, and
  Lilypad performs two of them on every LAN session — "Making an outgoing TCP
  connection: yes" and "Sending a UDP unicast: yes" — because that is what an
  ICE host candidate to a phone on the same Wi-Fi is.

  Apple's guidance on the key itself: "Any app that uses the local network,
  directly or indirectly, should include this description. This includes apps
  that use Bonjour and services implemented with Bonjour, as well as direct
  unicast or multicast connections to local hosts."
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/information-property-list/nslocalnetworkusagedescription

  Without it the system still shows the alert — it just shows it with no reason
  attached, on the one prompt whose answer decides whether the product's core
  claim ("nothing leaves your home") is true for that user.

  NSBonjourServices lists the `_lilypad._tcp` service type Lilypad uses on the
  LAN. The desktop **advertises** it via mDNS; the phone **browses** for it when
  the cached LAN address fails (NETWORKING.md §3 step 2). iOS requires this key
  when browsing; macOS includes it for the same Local Network permission bundle.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Lilypad connects your iPhone to this Mac directly over your own Wi-Fi, so your screen never leaves your home network.</string>
    <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
    <array>
      <string>_lilypad._tcp</string>
    </array>
  </dict>
</plist>
